@startuml f4-router-dispatch
title F4 - Executor registers, signals ready, pulls task from Router wait list

note right
  Router is the task queue manager.
  Executors pull tasks from Router's wait list.
  Router holds the executor pool state
  (registry, capacity, slots_in_use).
  Executors register once on startup,
  signal ready when they have free slots,
  and loop: pull next -> run -> post status -> ready.
end note

|EX (executor X)|
start
:On startup, publish executor.register to Router
 with routing_target, capacity, metadata;

|Router|
:Register executor X in pool with capacity N;
:Initialize slots_in_use = 0;

|EX (executor X)|
:Publish executor.ready to Router;

|Router|
:Mark executor as having N free slots;

|EX (executor X)|
:REST GET /tasks/next to Router (with routing_target);

|Router|
:Look up pending task
 where routing_target matches
 and slots_in_use less than capacity;
:If match: UPDATE task SET status=running, executor_id=X;
:slots_in_use = slots_in_use plus 1;
:Return task_spec and scope_token to EX;

note right
  EX can pull up to capacity N concurrent tasks.
  Each task = one container on the EX.
  capacity is the maximum number of
  concurrent containers the EX hosts.
end note

|EX (executor X)|
:Receive task_spec and scope_token;
:Pull pre-built agent image (OCI) if not cached;
:Prepare a new container runtime;

|AG|
:Container about to start;
:Read secrets via open env (see F5);
:Start agent loop (see F6);

note right
  When task completes (see F8),
  EX posts status=terminal to Router
  and signals ready again.
end note

stop

@enduml